Quick Chronicle update from my side.

Everything is now merged on main in:

https://github.com/pipavlo82/crystal-receipt

Current main commit:

e641d3c3

What is now pinned:

1. Pairwise checkpoint continuity specification

docs/CHRONICLE_CHECKPOINT_CONTINUITY_V0.md

This defines the exact question:

After applying shape and local-verification prerequisites, does the current chronicle_checkpoint.v0 validly continue the supplied predecessor candidate?

The result keeps separate axes for:

- evaluated
- unverifiable
- malformed
- not_evaluated

and only permits valid / invalid when continuity was actually evaluated.

It also separates:

- valid genesis;
- direct successor;
- predecessor-reference mismatch;
- sequence gap;
- same-sequence predecessor;
- higher-sequence predecessor;
- unknown predecessor;
- failed local verification.

2. Normative conformance vectors

tests/fixtures/chronicle-checkpoint-continuity-v0.json

There are exactly 15 machine-readable vectors covering the full v0 pairwise profile.

The vectors derive local verification through the existing verifyChronicleCheckpointV0 semantics rather than trusting declared intermediate booleans.

3. Chronicle documentation reference

docs/CHRONICLE.md

This now points to the separate continuity profile and states that the profile does not change:

- chronicle_checkpoint.v0;
- checkpoint root derivation;
- existing create* behavior;
- existing local verify* behavior.

Explicitly not claimed by this profile:

- global sequence uniqueness;
- duplicate or equivocation detection;
- observation-index semantics;
- entry-set superset monotonicity;
- collection_ref continuity;
- completeness or omission detection.

So this is specification and vectors only, not a claim that the cross-checkpoint verifier has already shipped.

What waits for Merlini after Lisbon:

- I implement the gate from the specification and vectors;
- Merlini independently implements the same gate without reading my implementation;
- we compare outcomes against all vectors byte-for-byte;
- any divergence identifies either an implementation bug or an ambiguity in the specification.

I deliberately stopped before implementation so the rule is fixed before either codebase gets to decide the semantics.
